# CRC for COBS Encoded Streams

In order to preserve the burst-detecting properties
of the CRC it must be calculated over the bits that
are sent over the lossy communication channel.

This means that the CRC should be computed *after*
COBS-encoding a stream. While this can easily be
done an obvious problem arises because the so computed
CRC may -- since it is not itself COBS encoded -- contain
the special comma characters (which we assume to
be 0x00 bytes here) and thus yield a stream that
is no longer properly COBS encoded:

COMMA, cobsencoded data, CRC, COMMA

where it is possible that the CRC contains
one or more COMMA characters.

## Using Guard Bits

We will show that this problem can be solved by
adding guard bits to the message. A particularly
simple case which we use here to demonstrate the
idea is the case where allocate 3 bytes for the
CRC added to the message. We designate two bits
of these 24 bits as guard bits. This leaves 22
bits for the CRC itself.

### Little-Endian Representation

For the following analysis we consistently assume
that bits are serially transmitted with the least-
significant bit first ('little-endian' bit ordering).

In this case the guard bits shall be the two least-
significant bits of the first byte assigned to
the CRC in the message.

### Role of the Guard Bits

The original (cobs-encocded) message is effectively
extended by two bits. We initially set these bits
to zero.

The 3 CRC bytes appended to the message have this
layout:

    CRC-bytes [0]:   CRC-bits[ 5 downto  0] : guard_bit_1 : guard_bit_0
    CRC-bytes [1]:   CRC-bits[13 downto  6]
    CRC-bytes [2]:   CRC-bits[21 downto 14]

### Amended CRC

Since the message has been extended by two bits 
these two bits must be added to the CRC computation as well:

   1. Compute CRC over all COBS-encoded bytes of the message
   2. Add two zero *bits* to the CRC:
       a) if ls-bit of CRC is one then right-shift the CRC 
          (by 1 bit) and XOR with the polynomial; otherwise
          right-shift only.
       b) repeat step a) one more time.

### Effect of setting one of the guard bits.

The CRC has the fundamental property that when
a message 'a' is XORed with a message 'b' then
the CRC of the so combined message is XOR of the
individual CRCs (any 'initial' value of the CRC
has to be properly taken into account):

    CRC(a XOR b) = CRC(a) xor CRC(b)

Thus, when we set one of the guard bits the CRC can
be XORed with the CRC of the respective guard bit!

These CRCs, however are very simple: 'guard_bit_1'
is technically the least-significant bit of the message;
the CRC is simply the polynomial itself!

Similarly, the CRC of the 'guard_bit_0' is the
polynomial right-shifted by one bit (note that
the rightmost bit spills into guard_bit_1 and
has to be properly XORed).

Another way to look at this is that the guard bit
is simply the highest-power bit of the polynomial
which is normally used implicitly. XORing the
any power of the full polynomial does not
change the CRC.

### Using the Guard Bits and Their CRC

As we have shown we perform any one or
both of the following operations without
affecting the total CRC of the message.

 1. set guard_bit_1 and XOR the CRC with the polynomial
 2. set guard_bit_0 and XOR the CRC with the polynomial
    right-shifted by one bit. Note that this right-shift
    spills into guard_bit_1; i.e. the final value
    of guard_bit_1 is inverted if guard_bit_0 is
    set AND the least-significant bit of the polynomial
    (the *stored* polynomial, i.e., the bit associated
    with the power 1) is set.

The idea now is that we have two operations
that can be used to modify the CRC in order to
avoid that any of the three bytes is a COMMA.

 1. Using any one or both of the operations above
    guarantees that at least one guard bit is set.
    Consequently, the first CRC byte is guaranteed
    *not* to be a comma.
 2. If the polynomial meets certain criteria then
    properly selecting one of the four possible choices
       a) do nothing (if amended CRC contains no COMMAs)
       b) XOR with guard_bit_1 and its CRC
       c) XOR with guard_bit_0 and its CRC
       d) XOR both guard bits and their CRCs
    allow us *always* to guarantee that none of the three
    CRC bytes are a COMMA byte.

### Constraints on Polynomial

Since we use the polynomial ('P') to 'spoil' the last two
CRC bytes it must not itself contain a COMMA:

   1. `P[21 downto 14] != 0x00`
   2. `P[13 downto  6] != 0x00`

The same must hold for the right-shifted polynomial
(corresponding to guard bit 0):

   3. `P[21 downto 15] != 0x00`
   4. `P[14 downto  7] != 0x00`

As well as their XOR

   5. `1. XOR 3. != 0x00`
   6. `2. XOR 4. != 0x00`

If we use an integer representation of the (little-endian)
polynomial then we can state this as follows:

     G1 = ((P<<1)|1)<<1
     G0 = ((P<<1)|1)<<0
     Gx = G0 XOR G1

     (G0 & 0xff0000) != 0
     (G1 & 0xff0000) != 0
     (Gx & 0xff0000) != 0
     (G0 & 0x00ff00) != 0
     (G1 & 0x00ff00) != 0
     (Gx & 0x00ff00) != 0

### Example

Consider the 22-bit polynomial (Koopman)

     P = 0x397e21

In this case

     G0 = (P<<2)|2 = 0xe5f886
     G1 = (P<<1)|1 = 0x72fc43
     G0 XOR G1     = 0x9704c5

So this polynomial meets the conditions above.

We proceed as follows:

     1. compute CRC over COBS-encoded message (using above polynomial)
     2. add two zero *bits* to this CRC.
     3. CRC <<= 2   (shift left; the two lsbits are the guard bits)
     4. IF (CRC & 0xff0000) != 0 AND (CRC & 0x00ff00) != 0 AND (CRC & 0x0000ff) != 0
        (no COMMA present) => SKIP to step 6.
     5. At least one byte is a COMMA; we must select one or both guards;
        either one of them will render the rightmost byte nonzero so
        we may focus on the leftmost two bytes.

        Note that XORing any combination of G0,G1 or both may modify the CRC
        such that one (or both) of the LHS bytes become zero; a situation
        that must and can be avoided. Consider, e.g.:

           (CRC & 0x0000ff) = 0x00 (RHS byte of CRC is a COMMA; must use guard)
           (CRC & 0xffff00) = (G0 & 0xffff00) (LHS bytes match G0 -> XORing G0 would
                                               make both bytes COMMAs)

        However, because the upper bytes match G0 and G0 XOR G1 meed the constraint
        we can simply XOR with G1 in this case and ensure that all three bytes are nonzero.

        another possibility is

           (CRC & 0x0000ff) = 0x00 (RHS byte of CRC is a COMMA; must use guard)
           (CRC & 0xff0000) = (G0 & 0xff0000) (LHS byte matches G0)
           (CRC & 0x00ff00) = (G1 & 0x00ff00) (MID byte matches G1)

        in this case a single guard is not enough since either one would render
        a COMMA either in the LHS or MID position; however, applying *both*
        guards avoids this in this scenario.

        Hence:
           if LHS matches G1:
              if MID matches G0:
                  => USE BOTH
              else
                  => USE G0
           elsif LHS matches G0:
              if MID matches G1:
                  => USE BOTH
              else
                  => USE G1
           else:
              if MID matches G1
                  => USE G0
              elsif MID matches G0
                  => USE G1
              else
                  => USE G0 (or G1, either choice works)

     6. Append       CRC & 0xff to the byte stream
     7. Append (CRC>> 8) & 0xff to the byte stream
     8. Append (CRC>>16) & 0xff to the byte stream

## Effect on the Receiver

Since the guard bits and their CRCs were manipulated in a way
consistent with the CRC's math by the sender the receiver does
not have to take any special action and may simply compute the
CRC over all bytes (including the appended three CRC bytes)
and check for the expected value.
